      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore you start, we recommend reading *Learning the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)* (<htcite0>), where you learned how to multiply by four and by five.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3bc9800d-aa41-4930-84f4-749eb6e40657",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Learning the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA multiplication table is like a map of answers: once we know the map, we no longer need to count everything again. Today we will learn two more of these maps — the table of six (6) and the table of seven (7). Remember from the previous text that multiplying by four and by five means adding the same number four or five times. The result of a multiplication is called the **product**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "These two tables also grow with small steps: counting by sixes (6, 12, 18, 24, 30...) and counting by sevens (7, 14, 21, 28, 35...). To learn the table of six we will look at a flower with six petals, and to learn the table of seven we will use the seven days of the week. Keep that counting rhythm in mind. Let's begin!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Growing by six and by seven</b><br /><br />Pause and think: does the table of six grow by six each time? Does the table of seven grow by seven each time?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: yes! Each time we move one step forward, the table of six adds six more (6, 12, 18, 24, 30) and the table of seven adds seven more (7, 14, 21, 28, 35). That is exactly the counting rhythm we will practice in the next sections.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_six",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Six (6)\n\nNow we turn to the table of six. Multiplying by six means adding the same number six times. Look at a flower: when it has six petals, its petals form a natural group of six. One flower shows 6 petals. Two flowers show 6 + 6 = 12 petals. We are counting by sixes: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30...",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 230\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A flower with six petals around its yellow center\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: A flower with six petals around its center. Each petal is one unit of our group of six.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Petals\n\nOne flower has 6 petals. Two flowers have 6 + 6 = 12 petals. Three flowers have 6 + 6 + 6 = 18 petals. Each new flower adds 6 more petals, so each step is one more multiplication: 6 × 1 = 6, 6 × 2 = 12, 6 × 3 = 18, and so on.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 240\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals\"><g><g transform=\"translate(-120 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(10 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#bbdefb\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(140 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#ffe0b2\" stroke=\"#ef6c00\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(270 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#e1bee7\" stroke=\"#6a1b9a\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(400 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#c8e6c9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Petals in a group</b><br />Look at Figure 2: it shows 5 flowers, and together they have 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 30 petals. Each flower has 6 petals. Suppose we pick a bunch of flowers and want to know how many petals they have in all. Try different numbers of flowers:<br />• 1 flower: 6 × 1 = 6 petals.<br />• 2 flowers: 6 × 2 = 12 petals.<br />• 4 flowers: 6 × 4 = 24 petals.<br />• 5 flowers: 6 × 5 = 30 petals.<br />Say the sequence out loud: six, twelve, eighteen, twenty-four, thirty. Do you see the pattern? Each new flower adds 6 more petals. That is what we call counting by sixes: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Six\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>6</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Multiplying any number by six is the same as adding it six times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “six times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The product is always N added to itself six times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by six at once.\n- For example, if **N** is 4, then 6 × 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 24.\n- **Tip**: because 6 = 3 × 2, the table of six is the table of three doubled: 3 × 4 = 12, so 6 × 4 = 24.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 6 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you hover, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch each answer of the table of six appear one by one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent6\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Is 6 × 3 equal to 3 × 2 × 3?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: we know that 6 = 3 × 2. Does 6 × 3 give the same result as 3 × 2 × 3?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: yes! The number 6 is a multiple of 3 and 2, because 3 × 2 = 6. So 6 × 3 is the same as (3 × 2) × 3; removing the parentheses gives 3 × 2 × 3 = 18. Counting by sixes three times also gives 6, 12, 18, so 6 × 3 = 18. The result is the same (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_seven",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Seven (7)\n\nNow we turn to the table of seven. Multiplying by seven means adding the same number seven times. A week has seven days, always in the same order (<htcite0>). The calendar selected at the top of the page shows how these days are named. One week has 7 days. Two weeks have 7 + 7 = 14 days. We are counting by sevens: 7, 14, 21, 28, 35...",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b504be6f-2380-4319-add3-c0682c07e886",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our Week",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"htCalendarFigureWrap\"><div id=\"htWeekCalendarFigure\" class=\"htCalendarFigure\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Seven-day calendar\"></div></div><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: The seven days of a week, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page. Each day is one unit of our group of seven.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "A seven-day week, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Days of the Week\n\nOne week has 7 days. Two weeks have 7 + 7 = 14 days. Three weeks have 7 + 7 + 7 = 21 days. Each new week adds 7 more days, so each step is one more multiplication: 7 × 1 = 7, 7 × 2 = 14, 7 × 3 = 21, and so on.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"htCalendarFigureWrap\"><div id=\"htFourWeekCalendarFigure\" class=\"htCalendarFigure\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Four-week calendar\"></div></div><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 4</b>: A calendar with four weeks, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "A four-week calendar, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 2: Days in a group of weeks</b><br />Look at Figure 4: it shows 4 weeks, and together they have 7 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 28 days. Each week has 7 days. Suppose we want to know how many days there are in several weeks. Try different numbers of weeks:<br />• 1 week: 7 × 1 = 7 days.<br />• 2 weeks: 7 × 2 = 14 days.<br />• 4 weeks: 7 × 4 = 28 days.<br />Say the sequence out loud: seven, fourteen, twenty-one, twenty-eight, thirty-five. Do you see the pattern? Each new week adds 7 more days. That is what we call counting by sevens: 7, 14, 21, 28!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Seven\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>7</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Multiplying any number by seven is the same as adding it seven times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “seven times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The product is always N added to itself seven times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by seven at once.\n- For example, if **N** is 5, then 7 × 5 = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 35.\n- **Tip**: each product of seven is the matching product of six plus N: 6 × 3 = 18, so 7 × 3 = 18 + 3 = 21.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 7 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you hover, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch each answer of the table of seven appear one by one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent7\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: How much is 7 × 6?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: count by sevens six times — 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42 — or use the table of seven to find the answer.<br /><br />Then check: is the result the same as 6 × 7?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: counting by sevens six times gives 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, so 7 × 6 = 42. And 6 × 7 = 42 too (<htcite0>): counting by sixes seven times gives 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, 42. The answer stays the same when we change the order of the factors.",
               "source": [
